    "slug": "emergency-boarding-up-glazing-gb",
    "title": "Smashed Window? The UK Guide to Emergency Glazing & Boarding",
    "content": "\r\n**A smashed window or a kicked-in door panel is a primary security breach that invalidates most UK home insurance policies if left unattended. In the UK's dense urban environments, an unsecured property is a magnet for squatters and vandals. This is the definitive 2025 UK Guide to Emergency Boarding Up, non-destructive uPVC security, and your legal obligations under the LASPO Act.**\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 1. The UK Context: uPVC vs. Timber Frames\r\nThe majority of UK residential properties built after 1980 utilize uPVC (Unplasticized Polyvinyl Chloride) window and door frames.\r\n*   **The Structural Difficulty:** uPVC frames are hollow and reinforced with internal steel. If you screw plywood directly into the white plastic, you destroy the thermal integrity and the internal drainage channels. Once a uPVC frame is drilled, it usually needs to be replaced entirely, turning a ┬ú150 glass repair into a ┬ú1,000 window replacement.\r\n*   **The Solution:** You must use the \"Batten\" or \"Sandwich\" method, which clamps the boards onto the frame without using screws.\r\n\r\n## 2. The \"Sandwich\" Boarding Method (Non-Destructive)\r\nThis is the professional standard for UK emergency glaziers.\r\n1.  **The Preparation:** Cut two sheets of 9mm or 12mm OSB/Plywood. One sheet for the inside, one for the outside.\r\n2.  **The Overlap:** The boards should be approximately 50mm (2 inches) wider than the opening on all sides.\r\n3.  **The Battening:** Place 2x1 timber battens horizontally across the middle of both boards.\r\n4.  **The Bolt:** Drill a hole through the center of the wooden \"sandwich.\" Pass a **Coach Bolt** (long carriage bolt) from the outside through to the inside.\r\n5.  **The Clench:** Tighten a nut on the inside. This compresses the boards against the frame, creating a secure, watertight seal with zero damage to the plastic or wood.\r\n\r\n## 3. High-Security Options: Steel Screening (Sitex)\r\nFor empty properties or those in high-crime areas of London or Birmingham, plywood is often not enough.\r\n*   **Sitex / Perforated Steel:** These are pre-cut steel screens that \"clench\" over doors and windows. They are fire-proof and cannot be cut with a standard saw.\r\n*   **The Benefit:** They allow airflow and light (perforated), preventing \"Damp\" and \"Mold\" which is a common issue with long-term plywood boarding in the UK's humid climate.\r\n\r\n## 4. Legal Context: Squatters & The LASPO Act 2012\r\nUnderstanding your rights is critical when a property is breached.\r\n*   **Section 144 of LASPO:** In 2012, the law changed to make squatting in a *residential* building a criminal offense. If you find people in your home after a window has been smashed, the police have the power to arrest and remove them immediately.\r\n*   **Commercial Loophole:** If you own a shop or office, squatting is still a civil matter. You must get a court order for eviction. Boarding up a commercial window is your #1 defense against a 6-month legal battle costing thousands in \"Interim Possession Orders.\"\r\n\r\n## 5. Insurance: The \"Reasonable Precautions\" Clause\r\nMost UK policies (Aviva, Direct Line, Admiral) contain a clause requiring the policyholder to take \"all reasonable precautions to avoid loss or damage.\"\r\n*   **The Security Breach:** If you leave a smashed window for 48 hours and the house is subsequently burgled, the insurer may argue you failed to take reasonable precautions and reject the claim.\r\n*   **The Board-Up Reimbursment:** Most insurers will cover the \"Trace and Access\" or \"Emergency Mitigation\" costs of boarding up. Always call your insurer's 24-hour helpline first; they often have \"Approved Boarding Contractors\" who bill the insurer directly.\r\n\r\n## 6. Case Study: The Bristol \"Nightmare Tenant\"\r\n*   **The Scenario:** After an eviction, a tenant smashed the glazed panels in the back door. The landlord taped a heavy-duty bin bag and some cardboard over the hole.\r\n*   **The Outcome:** It rained for three days. The cardboard turned to pulp. Water entered the kitchen, ruining the MDF cabinets.\r\n*   **The Cost:** $4,500 for a new kitchen. The insurer rejected the claim because the \"temporary repair\" was deemed insufficient to protect the property.\r\n*   **The Lesson:** Use wood. Tapes and plastics are only for 30 minutes of protection while you drive to B&Q.\r\n\r\n## 7. UK Emergency Boarding Costs (2025 Estimates)\r\n\r\n| Service / Material | Estimated Cost | Notes |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| **Plywood/OSB Sheet (B&Q)** | ┬ú20 ÔÇô ┬ú35 | DIY Material only |\r\n| **Emergency Callout (9pm - 6am)**| ┬ú180 ÔÇô ┬ú350 | Includes callout and 1hr labor |\r\n| **Security Shutter Rental** | ┬ú50 ÔÇô ┬ú100/week | For empty properties |\r\n| **Replacement DGU (Small)** | ┬ú150 ÔÇô ┬ú250 | Sealed double-glazed unit |\r\n| **Coach Bolt Set (Sandwich fix)**| ┬ú10 ÔÇô ┬ú15 | Essential for uPVC frames |\r\n\r\n## 8. The 12-Month UK Glazing Security Calendar\r\n*   **January:** Check for condensation between the panes (misted units). This means the argon gas has escaped, and the window is no longer \"insulating.\"\r\n*   **March:** Clean window tracks. Grit in the tracks leads to forced opening and shattered glass.\r\n*   **June:** Replace any cracked \"beading\" (the strips that hold the glass in).\r\n*   **October:** Check the PIR sensors on your outdoor security lights. Deterrence is cheaper than boarding.\r\n*   **December:** Never leave Christmas tree lights on \"extension leads\" through a window gap; it's an invitation to burglars and a stress point for the glass.\r\n\r\n## 9. Specialized UK Glazing Glossary\r\n1.  **Beading:** The plastic strips that \"click\" into the frame to hold the glass.\r\n2.  **Coach Bolt:** A bolt with a smooth exterior head used for security boarding.\r\n3.  **DGU:** Double Glazed Unit (the sealed glass \"sandwich\").\r\n4.  **Espag:** The metal locking mechanism inside a uPVC handle.\r\n5.  **Gasket:** The rubber seal between the glass and the frame.\r\n6.  **K-Glass:** Heat-reflecting glass (Standard in UK since 2002).\r\n7.  **Laminated Glass:** Two sheets of glass with plastic between (will not fall out when smashed).\r\n8.  **Sash Jammer:** A small rotating arm that adds a second locking point to a door or window.\r\n9.  **Spacer Bar:** The metal/plastic strip between the two panes of glass.\r\n10. **Toughened Glass:** Safety glass that breaks into thousands of tiny pebbles.\r\n\r\n## 10. Master FAQ: Top UK Homeowner Questions\r\n**Q: Can I board up a window from the inside?**\r\nA: **Yes, but it's less secure.** If you board from the inside, a burglar can still stand outside and work on the frame. If you have no choice (e.g., 3rd floor flat), use heavy-duty framing screws into the wooden frame, or the \"Sandwich method\" through the open sash.\r\n\r\n**Q: Does emergency boarding include glass removal?**\r\nA: **Yes.** Professional board-up companies will \"de-glaze\" the frame (remove the old shards and beads) before boarding. This makes the eventual glass replacement much faster and safer.\r\n\r\n**Q: How long can I leave the boards up?**\r\nA: **Technically, forever, but practically, 2-3 months.** Boards eventually warp and rot. More importantly, boards are a \"Blight\" on the street and can attract complaints to the local council's environmental health or planning departments.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n**Meta Title:** Emergency Boarding Up Guide UK | uPVC Security & Law 2025\r\n**Meta Description:** Smashed window? Learn the \"Batten Method\" to secure uPVC frames without damage. UK guide to insurance rules, squatter laws, and boarding costs.\r\n**Keywords:** emergency boarding up UK, broken window security, uPVC window repair, sandwich boarding method, squatter laws UK, window boarding cost, emergency glazier.\r\n"
  },
