    "slug": "ac-heat-pump-not-cooling-troubleshooting-gb",
    "title": "Heat Pump / AC Not Cooling? The UK Guide to F-Gas & Inverter Faults",
    "content": "\r\n**Coming home to a sweltering lounge? UK summers are becoming hotter and longer, making AC and Heat Pumps a vital part of domestic comfort. However, UK systems (predominantly \"Mini-Splits\") have specific maintenance needs. This is the definitive 2025 UK Guide to troubleshooting your AC or Heat Pump, understanding the F-Gas regulations, and identifying when a \"Deep Clean\" is the only fix.**\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 1. The UK Context: Mini-Splits and Heat Pumps\r\nUnlike the US, where \"Central Air\" is the norm, the UK relies on \"Mini-Split\" systems (Daikin, Mitsubishi, Toshiba). These units are both Air Conditioners and Air-Source Heat Pumps, meaning they can provide 4kW of heat for every 1kW of electricity they consumeÔÇömaking them 400% efficient.\r\n\r\n### The \"F-Gas\" Reality\r\nIn the UK, it is a criminal offense for an unlicensed person to open the refrigerant circuit of an AC unit. Only **REFCOM** or **F-Gas** certified engineers can legally handle gases like R32. As a homeowner, your troubleshooting is limited to \"Airflow\" and \"Power.\"\r\n\r\n## 2. Diagnosis 1: The \"Dirty Filter\" Lockout\r\n90% of UK residential AC call-outs are caused by lack of cleaning.\r\n*   **The Symptom:** The unit runs for 10 minutes, but the air isn't cold, or light on the front starts flashing.\r\n*   **The Cause:** Dust has clogged the mesh filters inside the indoor unit. This prevents air from crossing the cold \"Evaporator Coil.\" The coil then freezes into a block of ice, and the system shuts down to protect the compressor.\r\n*   **The Fix:** Lift the front flap of the indoor unit. Slide out the plastic mesh filters. Wash them under a lukewarm tap with a tiny bit of dish soap. Dry them thoroughly and slide them back in. **Do this every 3 months.**\r\n\r\n## 3. Diagnosis 2: The \"Sour Sock\" Smell (Biofilm)\r\nWhen an AC unit cools, it removes moisture from the air. This water sits in a \"Drip Tray\" inside the unit.\r\n*   **The Problem:** Dust and humidity create a breeding ground for bacteria and mold. This creates a gelatinous \"Biofilm\" that smells like old socks or damp vinegar.\r\n*   **The Risk:** Eventually, this gunk blocks the \"Condensate Drain Hole.\" The water then overflows the tray and runs down your wallpaper or onto your carpet.\r\n*   **The Pro Fix:** You need a \"Deep Clean\" (also called a \"Bagging Service\"). An engineer wraps the unit in a plastic bib and uses a low-pressure jet wash with alkaline chemicals to scrub the internal fins.\r\n\r\n## 4. Diagnosis 3: Outdoor Blockage\r\nIn the UK, we often hide our outdoor AC units behind \"Decorative Screens\" or in narrow \"Side Returns.\"\r\n*   **The Science:** The outdoor \"Condenser\" unit must be able to breathe. It needs at least 300mm of clearance at the back and 1 metre at the front.\r\n*   **The Failure:** If you stack garden furniture or bins in front of the unit, it can't dump its heat. The \"High Pressure\" safety switch will trip, and the indoor unit will blow warm air.\r\n*   **The Check:** Ensure the \"fins\" on the back of the outdoor unit aren't matted with cobwebs, dust, or autumn leaves. You can gently vacuum these or use a soft brush.\r\n\r\n## 5. Heat Pump Winter Maintenance: The \"Defrost\" Mystery\r\nUK homeowners often panic in January when they see \"Steam\" coming from their outdoor unit.\r\n*   **The Process:** To heat your house, the outdoor unit absorbs heat from the air. This makes the unit extremely cold (below freezing). Ice forms on the fins.\r\n*   **Defrost Mode:** Every 45ÔÇô60 minutes, the unit will reverse into \"Defrost Mode.\" It stops heating the house for 5 minutes to melt the ice on itself. This produces steam and a loud \"whooshing\" noise. This is perfectly normal.\r\n*   **The Warning:** Never pour boiling water over an iced-up unit. You can crack the copper pipes or trigger a thermal shock in the electronics.\r\n\r\n## 6. Case Study: The \"New Build\" Nightmare\r\n*   **The Scenario:** A homeowner in a new apartment in Reading complained the AC wasn't cooling.\r\n*   **The Investigation:** The engineer found the unit was \"Short Cycling\"ÔÇöturning on and off every 3 minutes.\r\n*   **The Discovery:** The \"Condensate Pump\" (a small device used to pump waste water uphill) had failed. \r\n*   **The Safety:** Modern AC units are wired so that if the pump fails, the AC won't run. This prevents a flood.\r\n*   **The Lesson:** If your AC won't turn on at all, it's often a ┬ú80 pump failure, not a ┬ú1,500 compressor failure.\r\n\r\n## 7. UK AC & Heat Pump Repair Costs (2025)\r\n\r\n| Service | Price Range (VAT Inc) | Summary |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| **Annual Maintenance Visit** | ┬ú120 ÔÇô ┬ú180 | Gas check + filter clean |\r\n| **Internal \"Deep Clean\"** | ┬ú150 ÔÇô ┬ú250 | Sanitising internal coil |\r\n| **Replace Condensate Pump** | ┬ú150 ÔÇô ┬ú250 | Parts and labour |\r\n| **Nitrogen Pressure Test** | ┬ú250 ÔÇô ┬ú400 | Finding a \"Ghost Leak\" |\r\n| **Re-gassing (R32)** | ┬ú150 + ┬ú40/kg | Refilling the system |\r\n| **Inverter PCB Replace** | ┬ú450 ÔÇô ┬ú850 | The main computer board |\r\n\r\n## 8. The 12-Month UK AC Maintenance Calendar\r\n*   **April:** Switch to \"Cooling Mode\" for 20 minutes to ensure the system is ready for the first heatwave.\r\n*   **June:** Clean internal filters before heavy summer use.\r\n*   **August:** Check the outdoor unit for \"weed growth\" or leaf blockages.\r\n*   **October:** Switch to \"Heating Mode.\" Check that the individual \"Louvres\" (the moving flaps) aren't sticking.\r\n*   **December:** In extreme cold, ensure the unit's drainage line (the white pipe outside) hasn't frozen solid.\r\n\r\n## 9. Specialized UK AC Glossary\r\n1.  **BTU:** British Thermal Unit (12,000 BTU = 3.5kW).\r\n2.  **COP:** Coefficient of Performance (Efficiency rating).\r\n3.  **Evaporator:** The indoor coil that gets cold.\r\n4.  **F-Gas:** The legal certification required to handle refrigerant.\r\n5.  **Inverter:** A technology that slows down the compressor rather than stopping/starting (saves 40% energy).\r\n6.  **Low-Ambient Kit:** Allows the AC to work in extreme cold.\r\n7.  **Mini-Split:** The standard wall-mounted UK system.\r\n8.  **R32 / R410A:** The types of gas used (refrigerants).\r\n9.  **REFCOM:** The professional body for AC engineers.\r\n10. **SEER:** Seasonal Energy Efficiency Ratio.\r\n\r\n## 10. Master FAQ: Top UK Homeowner Questions\r\n**Q: Can I use a \"DIY Regas Kit\" from Amazon?**\r\nA: **No.** These are illegal to use on domestic AC units in the UK. They often contain the wrong gas or moisture, which will internally corrode your compressor, turning a simple repair into a ┬ú2,000 replacement.\r\n\r\n**Q: My AC is \"misting\" (white fog coming out). Is it leaking?**\r\nA: **Usually, no.** If the air in the room is very humid and the AC is very cold, the moisture in the air condenses instantly into fog. This is common in the UK when first turning on the AC in a summer thunderstorm.\r\n\r\n**Q: How long does an AC unit last?**\r\nA: A high-quality Japanese brand (Mitsubishi/Daikin) should last 15ÔÇô20 years if serviced annually. Cheap \"rebranded\" units often fail at the 5ÔÇô7 year mark.\r\n\r\n**Q: Is \"Air Source Heat Pump\" the same as AC?**\r\nA: **A wall-mounted AC unit is an \"Air-to-Air\" heat pump.** It heats and cools the air directly. An \"Air-to-Water\" heat pump replaces your gas boiler and heats your radiators. Both use the same underlying technology.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n**Meta Title:** AC & Heat Pump Troubleshooting UK | Filters & Deep Cleaning 2025\r\n**Meta Description:** AC blowing warm air? Learn how to clean your filters, troubleshoot UK mini-split systems, and understand F-Gas laws and repair costs in 2025.\r\n**Keywords:** AC troubleshoot UK, heat pump not cooling, clean AC filters, F-gas laws UK, AC deep clean cost, mini split repair UK, air conditioning service.\r\n"
